From 8cd84ea7b9e2de899ae15eb4569f3ae5910048c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: htb_developer <htb_developer@securecoding.htb>
Date: Fri, 24 Jul 2026 11:54:23 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] restore the witness chain and bind every grant to the
 authenticated owner
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

## The vulnerability

`archive-service/src/chainReceipt.ts::verifyReceipt` was a stub that accepted any
`0x`-shaped JSON without consulting the chain, so a counterfeit receipt "no
rightful keeper issued" passed the weighing and `POST /archive/archive/open`
handed back the source grant (confirmed live). Worse, entitlement was derived
from **publicly observable on-chain material**: the app minted the bundle token
from public event fields and the registry's `unlockSeal` is a public value, so any
chain observer could obtain the source bundle.

## The fix

**1 — Weigh the claim against the witnessed filing (both services).** The
referenced transaction is read over RPC; the log at `receipt.logIndex` must have
been emitted by **the trusted registry address** (defeating a look-alike contract
re-emitting `ShardFiled`); the tx must have succeeded and be sufficiently
confirmed; the receipt's `owner`/`shardId` must equal the decoded event. Any
RPC/decode failure fails closed. A receipt with no matching event on the trusted
registry cannot survive the weighing.

**2 — Authorize on the authenticated owner, never on public chain material.** The
publicly emitted `unlockSeal` is treated as *provenance only*, not as an
entitlement. Authorization binds the verified on-chain `owner` to the
authenticated caller the trusted relay asserts (`x-archive-principal` /
`x-archive-role`): the archive `/open` and the app `/unlock` require the caller to
be a **keeper whose address is the witnessed owner**. The verified owner is the
only identity used at every grant point.

**3 — The bundle capability is not a bearer token.** `deriveArchiveToken` is an
HMAC keyed with the server-only `SOURCE_BUNDLE_SIGNING_KEY` (previously unused), so
it cannot be reproduced from public fields; the issued token is bound to the owner
it was granted to and is honoured only for that authenticated owner at
`/api/source/bundle`; and it is single-use, spent on presentation.

**4 — Single-use consumption bound to the witness.** Both grants are consumed once,
keyed by an id derived from the *witnessed* on-chain values, preserving exactly one
legitimate owner unlock; replays get `409`/`403`.

No contract change is required — the registry's public data is provenance, and the
services simply stop treating it as a secret.

## Distributed bundle rebuilt

`archive-service.locked.tar.gz` was still the pre-patch snapshot (stub
`verifyReceipt`); it is regenerated from the patched source so the shipped bundle
runs the hardened verifier, not the vulnerable one.

## Verification

- `npm run build` green; `npm test` green for both services with no prior build
  required (self-contained tests); `forge test` 3/3.
- End-to-end against a local anvil (production registry address): a keeper (owner)
  with a legitimate receipt unlocks and downloads the bundle; a caller that is a
  visitor, is not the owner, presents a forged/wrong-registry/rogue-emitter
  receipt, or replays a token/leaks it to a different principal is refused
  (`400`/`403`/`409`).

## Documented residual

Single-use / issued-token state is in-memory per-process (sufficient for this
single-instance deployment); production should persist it in a durable shared
store keyed by the witnessed receipt id.

